"""Authored readings of the fourteen captures of 2026-10-29: the entities of the provenance meta-aggregator, by name, on Google's
AI Overview. Each is checked against the census record of 2026-10-29 (provenanceerasure.org/aggregators/, data/provenance-census/
census.json, commit 8a9f153), whose fields carry a verbatim quote from the entity's own pages, saved with sha256."""
CK = "Checked 2026-10-29 against the provenance census record of the same day and the sources it saved."
A = {}
def a(q, **k): A[q] = k

a("IPTC photo metadata", ent="iptc-photo-metadata-tests",
  mt="IPTC PHOTO METADATA ON THE OVERVIEW — THE STANDARD COMPOSED (2025.1, CORE 1.5, EXTENSION 1.9, THE AI FIELDS); THE IPTC'S OWN TESTS OF WHETHER PLATFORMS KEEP IT ABSENT; 'SEARCH ENGINES READ EMBEDDED IPTC DATA'",
  d="IPTC PHOTO METADATA, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'A universally accepted industry standard for embedding administrative, descriptive, and copyright data directly into image files.' The answer gives the formats (JPEG, TIFF, PNG; IPTC Core and Extension; XMP), the difference from Exif, four categories (descriptive, administrative, rights, and 'AI & Content Provenance' with the 2025.1 fields AI System Used, AI System Version, AI Prompt Information, AI Prompt Writer Name), and three uses (asset management, Google Images, the IPTC inspector and GetPMD). Eleven cards, eight of them IPTC's own pages, with Wikipedia on the IIM, NeededApps, the Chrome Web Store and an IPTC video.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. Asked for IPTC photo metadata, the Overview explains the standard from the IPTC's own pages: what it holds, how it differs from camera data, how it has grown to describe images an AI system made, and the tools that read it.",
  findings=["THE STANDARD HELD. Core and Extension, IIM and XMP, the 2025.1 release and its AI properties are the IPTC's own (cards 2, 3 and 5). " + CK,
            "THE ADDRESS NAMES THE STANDARD; THE RECORD IS THE TESTS. The census row at this name is the IPTC Photo Metadata Working Group's tests of what platforms do to embedded metadata: Pexels, pixabay and Unsplash stripped it from saved and downloaded files, Flickr replaced the embedded creator with the account owner (2019). None of this is in the composition. At the bare name the Overview composes the carrier and leaves out the measurement of its erasure.",
            "'SEARCH ENGINES READ EMBEDDED IPTC DATA'. The composition says search engines read the data 'to display creator information and licensing attributes directly in search results', from IPTC's Google Images pages. Those pages say Google displays three IPTC fields 'wherever available', and warn that an 'asset management system, content management system, image management system or content delivery network may be stripping out embedded metadata fields'. The composition widens Google's display to search engines in general and drops the warning on the pages it drew on. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had said the IPTC's tests record the data 'often gone before any engine can read it'; the 2019 tests measured image-sharing platforms, and the hosting pipelines Google Images reads from were outside them.)",
            "THE AI FIELDS GIVEN AS PROVENANCE. 'AI & Content Provenance' is the composition's heading for the 2025.1 properties; the IPTC announcement (card 5, 27 November 2025) gives them as properties of AI-generated content."],
  analysis="At the bare name the composition layer gives the standard at the grain of its own documentation and leaves the census row's object, the record of the standard's erasure in transit, unsaid. The record keeps the tests as its unit; the capture is the baseline for the name, and shows that the measurement of stripping is a second address.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="The IPTC named; no person; no identifier; the IPTC's own pages cited (eight of eleven cards).")

a("IPTC Metawatch", ent="iptc-metawatch",
  mt="IPTC METAWATCH ON THE OVERVIEW — MONTHLY, 500+ PUBLISHERS IN 122 COUNTRIES, RIGHT; THE FINDINGS (7.2% WITH CREDIT; 321 OF 425 KEEPING NONE; 27 C2PA MANIFESTS IN 6,580) ABSENT; CARDS DATE IT TO SEPTEMBER",
  d="IPTC METAWATCH, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a free and open public monitoring tool that tracks how news publishers handle photo metadata.' Four features: monthly crawls, over 500 publishers in 122 countries, the fields tracked (credits, captions, copyright notices, Exif, IPTC and XMP), a per-field breakdown. Six cards, five the IPTC's (the announcement of 5 October 2026, the overview, the per-field page, a September events post, the photo-metadata archive), one LinkedIn post by Paul Melcher dated 14 September 2026.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview names the maker, the method and the scale of a monitor announced three weeks before, and points to its breakdown by field.",
  findings=["THE METHOD AND THE SCALE RIGHT. 'Every month', 'more than 500 news publishers in 122 countries' and the fields are the announcement's own (the census quotes 508 publishers and up to 20 recent articles each). 'Free and open' matches the record's licence quote: every run is published as open data under CC BY 4.0. " + CK,
            "THE FINDINGS ABSENT. The monitor exists to say what survives: in the October run 7.2% of editorial images carried a credit or copyright notice (8% in IMATAG's 2018 test), 321 of the 425 publishers it could crawl kept none of the scored IPTC fields, 27 of 6,580 images carried a C2PA manifest. The composition gives what the monitor checks and none of what it found.",
            "THE DATE. The record dates the monitor by its announcement, 5 October 2026. Two cards date it earlier: an IPTC post of 8 September 2026 in which Brendan Quinn 'appeared again to introduce Metawatch', and Paul Melcher's LinkedIn post of 14 September 2026 ('now publishes Metawatch'). The monitor is older still: the announcement counts 'the 294 publishers we have measured every month since June', and the monitor's pages date its first run 2026-06-03. Run from June, shown in September, announced 5 October; the record's lifecycle carries only the last. (Repaired 2026-10-29 from the pages the /non row saved; the LinkedIn post remains unread.)",
            "THE ACCESS LIMIT ABSENT. The announcement's note that AP and USA Today began blocking automated access in October, 'bot-blocking aimed at AI also shuts out research', is not composed."],
  analysis="A new provenance monitor is composed at the grain of its feature list. What the monitor measures is the erasure of credit in the publishing pipeline; the composition carries the instrument and leaves out its reading. The pattern is the IPTC photo metadata capture's in small: the carrier composed, the loss omitted.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="The IPTC named; Paul Melcher and Brendan Quinn appear only in cards; no identifier; the monitor's own pages cited.")

a("Nanopublications", ent="nanopublications",
  mt="NANOPUBLICATIONS ON THE OVERVIEW — ASSERTION, PROVENANCE, PUBLICATION INFO, FROM NANOPUB.NET; 'MILLIONS' CREATED, UNSOURCED IN THE RECORD; THE SIGNING AND THE SECOND-GENERATION SERVICES UNSAID",
  d="NANOPUBLICATIONS, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'the smallest independent unit of publishable scientific information, represented as a machine-readable knowledge graph snippet rather than a traditional research paper.' The structure in three parts (assertion, provenance, publication info), four features (machine-readable in RDF and OWL, citable and attributed on a decentralized network, a life-sciences focus with 'millions' created, nanopub-py and the nanopublications site). Nine cards: nanopub.net, the nanopub-py repository, the retired Research Object page, IEEE Xplore, an NIH-hosted paper, Research Ideas and Outcomes, the Pensoft blog, the nanopub documentation, a Medium essay.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview defines the unit, gives its three parts, says where it is used and how to make one.",
  findings=["THE MODEL HELD. The definition and the three parts are nanopub.net's: provenance 'describes how the assertion above came to be'; publication info is 'when and by whom it was created and the license terms for its reuse' (census quotes). " + CK,
            "'MILLIONS'. The record gives no count. The figure is at the grain of card 5 ('There are millions of nanopublications currently available on the Web, especially in the life science domain'); the composition's 'heavily concentrated in fields like genetics, pharmacology, and bioinformatics' raises 'especially' and names three disciplines no card names. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had called the figure unchecked.)",
            "WHAT IS NOT GIVEN. The second-generation Registry and Query services, which the record carries from nanopub.net's architecture page (updated 2026-10-28), are absent, as is the legacy server that keeps old unsigned nanopublications. Signing is absent too; the record carries it only by implication ('old (unsigned) nanopublications'), though the page it saved names it.",
            "A RETIRED PAGE AMONG THE CARDS. The Research Object card carries its own notice that the RO-Crate site superseded it after 2020 and that the pages are no longer maintained."],
  analysis="The model is composed from its own definition at full grain. The record's two negative tags, that the model carries provenance without measuring its survival and that publication does not settle an assertion's truth, are neither stated nor contradicted: the composition says 'citable and attributed' and stops there.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": False, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="No person or institution named in the answer (Groth et al. 2010 appears only in a card); no identifier; nanopub.net cited.")

a("C2PA Conformance Program", ent="c2pa-conformance",
  mt="THE C2PA CONFORMANCE PROGRAM ON THE OVERVIEW — GENERATORS, VALIDATORS, CERTIFICATE AUTHORITIES, THE EXPLORER; 'END-TO-END TAMPER EVIDENCE' GIVEN, THE PROGRAM'S OWN LIMIT (NO JUDGEMENT OF TRUTH; NO IDENTITY) LEFT OUT",
  d="THE C2PA CONFORMANCE PROGRAM, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a risk-based governance and verification framework run by the Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity to ensure hardware and software products properly implement the Content Credentials specification.' Components (the Conforming Products List on the Conformance Explorer, generator products at assurance Level 1 or 2, validator products, certificate authorities and the Trust List), the process (expression of interest, agreements, evidence, self-tests) and trust ('only conforming generator products can receive production claim-signing certificates', 'end-to-end tamper evidence'). Eight cards: C2PA's conformance page and FAQ, two Content Authenticity Initiative pages, the Explorer, SSL.com, Trufo (9 October 2026), Scott Perry on LinkedIn (31 July 2026, the v0.2 documents).",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview says what the program certifies, who takes part, how a product is admitted and why admission matters for signing.",
  findings=["THE PROGRAM HELD. Its purpose is the conformance page's ('producing and validating C2PA data correctly'); the products list is its unit; v0.2 appears through Scott Perry's card. 'Risk-based governance' is C2PA's own phrase: the conformance page names 'a risk-based, transparent and unbiased governance process', and card 7, hosted in Trufo's repository, is C2PA's program document, whose glossary opens 'A risk-based governance program'. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had given the phrase to Trufo.) " + CK,
            "THE LIMIT LEFT OUT. The record's negative tags are C2PA's own: Content Credentials 'do not provide value judgments about whether a given set of provenance data is \"true\"', and 'The C2PA specification does not directly address the topic of human or organizational identity'. The composition gives 'end-to-end tamper evidence for digital media' and omits both; the FAQ card's snippet ('not DRM') is the nearest it comes.",
            "THE COUNT. The conforming-products JSON list held 286 records when the record read it; the composition gives no count."],
  analysis="The program is composed as its applicants meet it: components and steps. The program's statement of what conformance cannot establish, the half of the record that bears on provenance, does not reach the composition. A reader leaves with tamper evidence and without the caveat that well-formed provenance can be false.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="C2PA named; no person in the answer (Scott Perry in a card); no identifier; C2PA's own pages cited.")

a("Data Provenance Initiative", ent="data-provenance-initiative",
  mt="THE DATA PROVENANCE INITIATIVE ON THE OVERVIEW — 'LED IN PART BY RESEARCHERS FROM MIT'; FOUR PLATFORMS WHERE THE RECORD HAS ONE; THE 70% OMISSION AND 50% ERROR RATES RIGHT; THE HOME PAGE'S 4,000+ DATASETS IN A CARD",
  d="THE DATA PROVENANCE INITIATIVE, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a multi-institutional research project—led in part by researchers from MIT—that audits the datasets, licenses, and web domains used to train artificial intelligence models.' Four platforms (the Data Provenance Explorer over 1,800+ datasets, a Web Consent Explorer, Open Model Evolution, an AI Observatory) and three findings (license omission over 70% and error over 50% on hosting platforms, closed datasets concentrated in creative tasks and lower-resource languages, a rise of non-commercial licences driven by synthetic data). Four cards: the initiative's home page ('4,000+ audited datasets; 14,000 tracked web domains'), the arXiv paper, MIT Sloan, Nature Machine Intelligence (1,858 datasets across 44 collections).",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview identifies the project, lists its tools and reports what its audits found, from its own site, its paper and the press.",
  findings=["THE FINDINGS RIGHT. 'license omission of 70%+ and error rates of 50%+' is the record's quote from the founding paper. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had given the README as its source.) " + CK,
            "THE OPERATOR. The record quotes the initiative's own description, 'a multi-disciplinary volunteer effort'; the composition's 'led in part by researchers from MIT' follows the MIT Sloan card, and 'multi-institutional' is in no card. The record names the founding paper's authors (Shayne Longpre and 16 others); the composition names no one.",
            "THE RECORD AT TOO COARSE A GRAIN. The record has one explorer and the first release's count (44 collections, 1,800+ datasets). The home-page card gives four platforms and '4,000+ audited datasets; 14,000 tracked web domains'. The /non row read dataprovenance.org the same day: 'Four public measurement systems' (the Data Provenance Explorer, the Web Consent Explorer, Open Model Evolution, the AI Observatory) and '4,000+ / datasets audited', '14,000 / web domains tracked'. The card's figures hold; the record is to carry them with their quotes.",
            "THE NEGATIVE TAGS. 'NOT legal advice' and 'describing a collection does not establish which model used it' are not composed; the composition does not claim otherwise."],
  analysis="As with the revived Papers with Code on the MMRS side, the composition is ahead of the record on the entity's current extent: the census formalized the initiative from its first release and its repository, and the Overview read its present home page. The capture is a correction to be made from the entity's own page.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="MIT named; no person; no identifier; the initiative's home page and paper cited.")

a("AttributionBench", ent="attributionbench",
  mt="ATTRIBUTIONBENCH ON THE OVERVIEW — OSU'S BENCHMARK EXACT: SEVEN SOURCE DATASETS, THE BINARY TASK, ABOUT 80% MACRO-F1; KEPT APART FROM ATTRIBENCH",
  d="ATTRIBUTIONBENCH, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a comprehensive research benchmark designed to evaluate automatic attribution and citation faithfulness in Large Language Model (LLM) and generative search engine responses.' A unified testbed of seven datasets (ExpertQA, Stanford-GenSearch, AttributedQA, LFQA, BEGIN, HAGRID, AttrEval-GenSearch), the task (is every claim fully supported by its cited evidence: attributable or not), the data on Hugging Face and GitHub; findings (about 80% macro-F1 for GPT-4 and fine-tuned GPT-3.5, 60–70% on harder sets; over 66% of errors from fine-grained information insensitivity, nearly 27% from a mismatch of access and context). Five cards: arXiv 2402.15089, the OSU-NLP-Group repository, the project page, the Hugging Face dataset, the ACL Anthology.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview identifies the benchmark, what it compiles, what it asks and how hard models find it, all from the paper and its repositories.",
  findings=["THE ENTITY EXACT. The task ('whether every claim within the generated responses is fully supported by its cited evidence') and the 80% macro-F1 are the record's quotes from the project page. " + CK,
            "KEPT APART. The composition never mentions AttriBench, the quote-attribution benchmark with a near name; the record's negative tag ('NOT AttriBench') is respected by silence.",
            "THE AUTHORS IN THE CARDS ONLY. Yifei Li, Xiang Yue, Zeyi Liao and Huan Sun are named in the arXiv and Hugging Face cards; the answer names neither them nor the OSU NLP Group.",
            "THE ERROR SHARES. The 66% and 27% figures are the paper's error analysis; on the project page the record saved ('Over 66%', 'About 26.8%'); the record does not carry them. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had called them unchecked.)"],
  analysis="An attribution benchmark is composed at full grain from its own paper and kept distinct from its homonym, and its makers are left in the cards. The record's negative tag, that support by evidence is not credit to the original author, is the line between this entity and the next.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": False, "id": True, "src": True}, per_note="No person or institution in the answer; the arXiv identifier is in a card; the paper, repository and dataset cited.")

a("AttriBench", ent="attribench",
  mt="ATTRIBENCH ON THE OVERVIEW — THREE HOLDERS OF ONE NAME: THE 2026 QUOTE-ATTRIBUTION BENCHMARK (SUPPRESSION NAMED), A 2023 PYTORCH XAI LIBRARY, A FINANCE EXERCISE; THE AUTHORS UNNAMED",
  d="ATTRIBENCH, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'The term AttriBench primarily refers to two distinct tools.' First, 'Released in 2026, AttriBench is the first fame- and demographically-balanced quote attribution benchmark dataset for Large Language Models', with its findings (disparities across race, gender and intersectional groups; 'suppression', omission of attribution though the model has the authorship data) and the Hugging Face repository. Second, attribench, a 2023 PyTorch library for benchmarking feature-attribution methods (PyPI, Read the Docs). A note: in finance, AttriBench is a Brinson-attribution exercise. Five cards: arXiv 2604.05224, PyPI, Read the Docs, the bermaneh/AttriBench dataset, Eureka Simulations.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The name belongs to three things, and the Overview separates them: the new LLM benchmark first, an older explainable-AI library second, a finance exercise in a note, each with its own source.",
  findings=["THE ENTITY HELD AND FIRST. 'the first fame- and demographically-balanced quote attribution benchmark dataset' is the abstract verbatim (the record's quote); suppression is the record's second relation type. " + CK,
            "THE HOMONYMS NAMED AND SOURCED. The PyTorch library (PyPI; the card's date, 25 August 2023, is release 0.1.4's, the first release 2023-07-26) and the finance exercise (Eureka Simulations) are real and distinct, each carried by its own card. The record had one negative tag, 'NOT OSU's AttributionBench'; the capture adds two homonyms the record did not know. The record is to carry them as negative tags.",
            "THE AUTHORS UNNAMED. Eliza Berman, Bella Chang, Daniel B. Neill and Emily Black are in the record; the answer names no one, and only the dataset path 'bermaneh/AttriBench' carries a trace of the first author.",
            "WHAT IS NOT GIVEN. The scale (15,620 quotes from 6,292 authors), the eleven models tested, acceptance at COLM 2026 and the fame proxy (Google Search hit counts) are absent."],
  analysis="Asked a name with three holders, the composition disambiguates and puts the provenance benchmark first; the disambiguation is sourced and clean. The benchmark measures suppression, the omission of attribution where authorship is known, and its own composition here omits its four authors: a small instance of the failure it names.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": False, "id": True, "src": True}, per_note="No person or institution named in the answer; the arXiv identifier in a card; the paper and dataset cited.")

a("ProvStore", ent="provstore",
  mt="PROVSTORE ON THE OVERVIEW — THE W3C PROV REPOSITORY IN THE PRESENT TENSE (RETIRED DECEMBER 2024, UNSAID; THE LANDING PAGE'S 'COMING BACK SOON' AMONG THE CARDS); 'OPEN PROVENANCE MODEL'; A SHIBUYA STREETWEAR SHOP AND AN EXAM BOOKSTORE",
  d="PROVSTORE, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'ProvStore most commonly refers to the Open Provenance Model / W3C PROV Public Repository, an online public provenance repository designed to store, browse, visualize, and manage data provenance documents using W3C standards via a web interface or REST API.' Then two local businesses with map panels: PROV Tokyo, a skate and streetwear shop in Shibuya (3.8, 50 reviews, 'Closed'), and the PROV Exam Bookstore in Sandy, Utah (4.2, 1.2K, 'Open'). The closing question offers the three. Six cards: the OpenProvenance page ('ProvStore is coming back soon'), PROV Exam, ePrints Soton and the ACM Digital Library (the paper), the PROV online store, a Google place listing.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The name is shared, and the Overview gives the research repository first and then two businesses called PROV, asking which is meant.",
  findings=["THE LIFECYCLE UNSAID. The record: 'Updated 12-Sep-2026 : ProvStore was retired in December 2024', from the maintainer's page. The composition describes the repository in the present tense, and its first card is the OpenProvenance landing page that still says 'ProvStore is coming back soon'. The record's negative tag is 'a live landing page is not a live repository'; the composition follows the landing page. " + CK,
            "'OPEN PROVENANCE MODEL / W3C PROV'. The Open Provenance Model is the community model that preceded W3C PROV; ProvStore's paper calls it 'the first online public provenance repository supporting the new PROV standards by W3C' (cards 3 and 4). The composition joins predecessor and standard into one name for the repository.",
            "THE HOMONYMS. PROV Tokyo and PROV Exam share the four letters PROV and no relation to the repository; the composition gives them with map panels, ratings and hours, equal in layout to the research entity. The record is to carry them as negative tags.",
            "THE MAINTAINER UNNAMED. Dong Huynh and King's College London are in the record; the answer names no one."],
  analysis="A retired provenance repository is composed as a live one: the composition takes the entity's state from the page that misstates it, and the dated retirement on the maintainer's page does not reach it. The state of the entity is the provenance fact the record exists to keep, and it is the one the composition drops.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": False, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="No person or institution named (W3C only as the standard); no identifier; OpenProvenance and the paper cited.")

a("WikiWho", ent="wikiwho",
  mt="WIKIWHO ON THE OVERVIEW — REPLACED BY WIKIHOW: JACK HERRICK, 2005, 100,000 ARTICLES, 19 CATEGORIES; THE TOKEN-LEVEL AUTHORSHIP TOOL FOR WIKIPEDIA ABSENT, AND NO NOTICE OF THE SUBSTITUTION",
  d="WIKIWHO, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'wikiHow is a globally collaborative, wiki-style website featuring an extensive database of human-written how-to guides, instructions, quizzes, and community forums.' Founded 2005 by Jack Herrick; over 100,000 articles; 19 top-level categories; a link to wikiHow's category listing; an offer to find a guide. The sources are chips: 'Wikipedia +3', 'Fastly +1', 'wikiHow +2', 'wikiHow +1'. WikiWho, the algorithm and API that attribute each word of a Wikipedia article to the revision and editor that introduced it, does not appear.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview takes the query as a near spelling of wikiHow and answers about wikiHow, sourced to Wikipedia and wikiHow's own pages.",
  findings=["SUBSTITUTION AT THE NAME. Every sentence of the composition is about wikiHow. The query is the entity's exact name; the record: 'The WikiWho API gives access to the provenance/changes of words (tokens) in Wikipedia articles' (its README), maintained since August 2021 by Wikimedia Community Tech and the Wiki Education Foundation. " + CK,
            "NO NOTICE. The paste carries no 'showing results for' line and no statement that the query was read as another name; the composition answers a different entity as if it were the one asked for.",
            "THE PROVENANCE TOOL ERASED BY ITS NEIGHBOUR. WikiWho attributes authorship in Wikipedia at the level of the word. At its own name the composition layer gives a how-to site sourced in part to Wikipedia; the instrument for crediting Wikipedia's writers is replaced by a more-retrieved name one letter away.",
            "THE FORM OF THE CAPTURE. Unlike the thirteen others, the paste is the Overview as shown, unexpanded: the query once, the source chips inline with their counts, no card rail. The operator's note with it: 'wikiwho gets replaced - good disambiguation on this one, and theyll owe us:'."],
  analysis="The cleanest erasure of the census: the entity is absent from the composition at its exact name, and its place is taken by a neighbour of higher retrieval weight, with no notice to the reader. The census record, with its verbatim self-description from WikiWho's README, the MediaWiki page and the Meta research page, is the disambiguation the composition did not make.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": False, "id": False, "src": False}, per_note="The entity is absent: no person, institution, identifier or page of WikiWho's; the persons and sources named are wikiHow's.")

a("Mapping Manuscript Migrations", ent="mapping-manuscript-migrations",
  mt="MAPPING MANUSCRIPT MIGRATIONS ON THE OVERVIEW — THE FOUR PARTNERS AND DIGGING INTO DATA RIGHT; 'OVER 215,000' MANUSCRIPTS; THE OXFORD CATALOGUE GIVEN AS 'THE MEDIEVAL MANUSCRIPTS CATALOGUE'; THE PROJECT'S END IN 2020 AND ITS LICENCE UNSAID",
  d="MAPPING MANUSCRIPT MIGRATIONS, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'an international digital humanities project that unites disparate historical records to trace the origins, movements, provenance, and ownership history of medieval and Renaissance manuscripts.' Scale ('over 215,000 historical manuscripts'), partners (Penn, Oxford, IRHT-CNRS, Aalto) and funding (the Trans-Atlantic Platform's Digging into Data Challenge), and the merged sources (the Schoenberg Database, Bibale, 'the Medieval Manuscripts Catalogue') as a linked open data knowledge graph, queryable through the portal and LDF.fi. Six cards: the GitHub page, the Journal of Open Humanities Data (2020), EADH, the University of San Diego, the Zenodo record of the knowledge graph, Goldsmiths Research Online.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview gives the project's aim, scale, partners, funder and the three databases it joins, and says where the data can be queried.",
  findings=["THE PROJECT HELD. Partners and funder are the record's; the aggregation of three databases into linked open data is its self-description. " + CK,
            "THE COUNT. 'over 215,000' follows the San Diego card ('more than 215,000 medieval'); the record's white-paper figures are 217,700 manuscripts and other entities in the portal and 262,000 source records combined.",
            "ONE SOURCE SHORTENED. The record names the three sources as 'the Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts, Medieval Manuscripts in Oxford Libraries, and Bibale'; two of the composition's cards (GitHub, San Diego) call the third 'the Medieval Manuscripts Catalogue at the University of Oxford', and the composition drops 'at the University of Oxford'. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had read it as the record's name with Oxford dropped.)",
            "WHAT IS NOT GIVEN. The licence (CC BY-NC 4.0), on the data-paper card, is absent; the Zenodo copy of the dataset appears as a card. The project's term (funded 2017–2020) is on no card, only in the white paper the composition did not surface. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had counted the term among what the cards offered.)"],
  analysis="An aggregator of provenance is composed with its sources and partners right and its own state and terms of reuse left out: a reader cannot tell from the composition that the project ended in 2020, a fact on no card, or that its data are licensed for non-commercial reuse, a fact on one. The grain is the project's own description; the record's lifecycle and licence fields carry what the composition does not.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="Penn, Oxford, IRHT-CNRS and Aalto named; no person; no identifier; the project's own GitHub page and data paper cited.")

a("Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts", ent="schoenberg-database",
  mt="THE SCHOENBERG DATABASE ON THE OVERVIEW — 240,000 OBSERVATIONS FROM 14,000 SOURCES; THE FIVE RECORD TYPES WITH THE ENTRY AS OBSERVATION; THE CC BY-SA LICENCE UNSAID",
  d="THE SCHOENBERG DATABASE OF MANUSCRIPTS, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a free, open-access, crowd-sourced digital research tool hosted by the Schoenberg Institute for Manuscript Studies at Penn Libraries.' Purpose (trace pre-modern manuscripts from creation to the present), scale ('over 240,000 manuscript observation records gathered from more. than 14,000' catalogues and inventories), period (500 to 1600) and region, export in .xlsx and .csv, and five record types (sources, entries, manuscript records, name authority, place authority). Eight cards: Medieval Digital Resources, Penn Libraries (three), a Bibliographical Society of America video, AWOL, Penn Press, Aalto's Semantic Computing Research Group.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview identifies the database and its host, says what it traces and from how many sources, and lays out its structure.",
  findings=["THE UNIT HELD. Entries as 'individual observations or listings extracted from those sources', linked under manuscript records: the record's unit, 'an observation of a manuscript in a sales or auction catalogue'. The two counts are the record's two, joined in one sentence: 14,000 sources from the home page, and 240,000 records as card 8 (the MMM data paper) gives them, 'more than 240,000 records for manuscript observations', a 2020 count composed in the present tense. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had credited the count to the white paper, which is not on the rail.) " + CK,
            "THE LICENCE REDUCED. Card 1 gives the terms as 'Must provide credit , No Fee' and card 6 reprints the CC BY-SA 4.0 sentence for downloaded results; the composition gives 'Free to use without a fee' and drops the credit condition and the share-alike licence.",
            "THE PERIOD. '500 to 1600' is card 1's ('Dates:  500 - 1600'). (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had called it unchecked.)",
            "THE PASTE AS RECEIVED. 'from more. than 14,000' carries a stray full stop inside the composition; kept."],
  analysis="Host, unit, scale and structure are composed at the database's own grain, from its own and its neighbours' pages. The conditions of reuse are reduced to the absence of a fee, the credit condition and the share-alike licence dropped, and a 2020 count is given as the present one.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="The Schoenberg Institute and Penn Libraries named; no person (Lawrence J. Schoenberg in cards); no identifier; Penn's pages cited.")

a("VroniPlag Wiki", ent="vroniplag-wiki",
  mt="VRONIPLAG WIKI ON THE OVERVIEW — 28 MARCH 2011, THE NAME FROM VERONICA SASS, THE NOTICE TO THE UNIVERSITY BEFORE PUBLICATION; THE WIKI'S OWN OVERVIEW CARD: 'DOKUMENTIERT 204 DISSE…'",
  d="VRONIPLAG WIKI, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a crowdsourced online platform hosted on Fandom that investigates and documents academic plagiarism in German doctoral dissertations, habilitations, and scientific publications.' Launch (28 March 2011), name (after Veronica Saß, daughter of Edmund Stoiber, the first case documented), scope (percentages of copied text, side-by-side comparisons, revocations), process (findings reported to the university's office of academic integrity before public release), recognition (the Zedler-Preis of Wikimedia Germany, 2012). Three cards: English Wikipedia, the wiki's home on Fandom, its Übersicht page ('Es dokumentiert 204 Disse…').",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview identifies the wiki, its founding and name, what it documents and how it proceeds, and points to its home and its overview.",
  findings=["THE ENTITY HELD. The date, Wikia (now Fandom) as host, the documentation of plagiarism in German theses and the notice to the university are the record's quotes from English Wikipedia. " + CK,
            "THE WIKI'S OWN COUNT, IN A CARD. The record could not read the wiki (403 to a plain fetch on 2026-10-29) and took its count from German Wikipedia: 201 dissertations and 13 habilitation theses, as of 3 April 2023. The Übersicht card is the wiki's own page, truncated: 'Es dokumentiert 204 Disse…'. The composition does not give the figure; the card carries a later count than the record's, from the source the record could not reach.",
            "WHAT IS NOT GIVEN. The method (each fragment classified and verified by a second person), the revocations (at least 90 doctorates, as of 30 April 2024) and the founder German Wikipedia names (Martin Heidingsfelder) are absent; 'the status of university degree revocations' gestures at the second.",
            "THE PRIZE. The Zedler-Preis of 2012 is in both sources the record saved (English and German Wikipedia) and in none of its fields. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had called it unchecked.)"],
  analysis="A plagiarism wiki, itself an instrument of attribution, is composed from Wikipedia with its founding and procedure right. The capture adds to the record the one figure the record lacked from the wiki itself, through a truncated card.",
  per_v={"author": True, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="Veronica Saß and Edmund Stoiber named (the first case, the name's origin); Wikimedia Germany and Fandom named; no identifier; the wiki's own pages cited.")

a("Getty Provenance Index", ent="getty-provenance-index",
  mt="THE GETTY PROVENANCE INDEX ON THE OVERVIEW — 12 MILLION RECORDS ON ARCHES, THE GETTY RESEARCH INSTITUTE, THE KNOEDLER AND GOUPIL STOCK BOOKS; THE FOUNDING (1974 OR THE EARLY 1980S, BURTON FREDERICKSEN) UNSAID",
  d="THE GETTY PROVENANCE INDEX, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'an open-access digital database maintained by the Getty Research Institute that provides over 12 million records tracking the ownership, circulation, display, and transfer of artworks.' Scope (12 million linked open data records on the Arches platform; archival inventories, auction catalogues and dealer stock books; the Knoedler and Goupil stock books; sales catalogues from roughly the sixteenth to the twentieth century), searchable categories, and the search portal. An image strip ('View all') heads the paste. Nine cards: three Getty pages, Princeton's guide to the legacy database, the GitHub CSV exports, CNI, USC Libraries, ARIAH (the relaunch), Smarthistory.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview identifies the index, its maintainer and its scale, says what documents it draws on and how to search it.",
  findings=["THE INDEX HELD. 'over 12 million' resources, the Getty Research Institute and the move to linked open data are the record's. " + CK,
            "THE FOUNDING UNSAID, AND WITH IT THE CONFLICT. The record holds two founding statements from the Getty's own pages: 'Founded in 1974 by Burton Fredericksen' and 'began as an initiative within the Getty Museum’s Paintings department in the early 1980s led by curator Burton B. Fredericksen'. The composition gives neither, and names no person.",
            "THE STOCK BOOKS. 'the 11 painting stock books of the New York dealership M. Knoedler & Co. and Goupil & Cie (covering 1872–1970)' joins two dealers' books under one count and one span. The Getty's coverage page, which the record saved, gives M. Knoedler & Co. (New York, 1872–1970) the 11 painting stock books and Goupil & Cie./Boussod, Valadon & Cie (Paris, 1846–1919) 15 stock books of their own. The composition also gives the sales catalogues the whole Index's span ('roughly from the 16th century through the 20th century'). (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had left the join unchecked.)",
            "THE LEGACY AND THE COVERAGE. The record's lifecycle (archival inventories still on the legacy platform during the transition) and its negative tag (coverage varies by region, period and type of document) are absent; the Princeton card's date ranges are the legacy database's."],
  analysis="The index of provenance is composed with its scale and maintainer right and its own origin left out. The founder is the person most associated with the index on the Getty's pages; the composition gives the institution and leaves the person in the archive.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="The Getty Research Institute, Knoedler and Goupil named; no person; no identifier; the Getty's own pages cited.")

a("OLMoTrace", ent="olmotrace",
  mt="OLMOTRACE ON THE OVERVIEW — AI2, REAL TIME, INFINI-GRAM; 'FACT-CHECKING: VERIFY WHETHER SPECIFIC CLAIMS … ARE BACKED BY REAL TRAINING DOCUMENTS', THE AUTHORS' USE GIVEN WITHOUT THEIR LIMIT",
  d="OLMOTRACE, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'an open-source system developed by the Allen Institute for Artificial Intelligence (Ai2) that traces language model outputs back to their multi-trillion-token training datasets in real time.' How it works (the Show OLMoTrace button highlights verbatim spans; matches against corpora such as Dolma, ranking up to 10 documents by BM25; an extended infini-gram at O(L log N)), supported models (OLMo 2 32B and 13B Instruct, OLMoE 1B 7B, the OLMo 3 family), uses (fact-checking, hallucination diagnosis, auditing). Six cards: Ai2's blog, arXiv 2504.07096, the ACL Anthology, Ai2 on X (OLMo 3), Techzine, Ken Yeung's newsletter.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview names the tool and its maker, explains how it finds training text in a model's answer, lists the models it covers and what it is good for.",
  findings=["THE TOOL HELD. Ai2, real time, multi-trillion tokens and verbatim matching are the paper's: 'OLMoTrace finds and shows verbatim matches between segments of language model output and documents in the training text corpora' (record). " + CK,
            "THE USE WITHOUT ITS LIMIT. The authors name fact checking as a use: 'We showcase how it can be used to explore fact checking' (abstract), and in their first case study 'we can use their corresponding documents to fact check claims made in the spans'. Their Limitations bound it: 'The retrieved documents should not be interpreted as having a causal effect on the LM output, or as supporting evidence or citations for the LM output' (the record's negative tags). The composition's first use is 'Fact-Checking: Verify whether specific claims in a model's answer are backed by real training documents': the use at a raised force, the limit dropped; Ai2's blog post, card 1, also omits the limit. (Repaired 2026-10-29: the finding had called fact checking a use the authors disclaim.)",
            "THE PARTNERS UNSAID. The record names the University of Washington, UC Berkeley and Stanford with Ai2; the composition names Ai2 alone.",
            "THE MODELS CURRENT. The OLMo 3 family comes from Ai2's post on X; the record's count is OLMo-2-32B-Instruct's training data (3.2 billion documents, 4.6 trillion tokens). The record is to carry OLMo 3 from Ai2's own page."],
  analysis="A provenance tool whose authors state precisely what a match does not mean is composed with the use they name and without the bound they set on it. The composition turns a trace of verbatim overlap into evidence for a claim, the conversion of a matching relation into a citation relation that the paper's Limitations refuse; the use is the authors', the conversion the composition's.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": True, "src": True}, per_note="Ai2 named; no person; the arXiv identifier in a card; Ai2's own post and the paper cited.")
